  "slug": "drosophila-epithelial-7721",
  "summary": "Recent work resolves epithelial death, debris clearance, matrix remodeling, and stromal guidance during regeneration and wound repair.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how epithelial cells coordinate death, clearance, and movement during tissue repair. In the adult Drosophila gut, hypoxia primes enterocytes for apoptosis via AMPK-p53 and activates Upd-JAK-STAT in stem cells, with regeneration proceeding in a reoxygenation-dependent manner. In wing discs, neighboring hinge epithelial cells clear apoptotic debris through Draper-dependent efferocytosis, and Draper loss delays clearance. Apical extracellular matrix in abdominal morphogenesis shifts from anchoring stationary larval epithelia to supporting directional migration. A bioengineered intestinal epithelial-fibroblast model shows fibroblast alignment and contact guiding organoid epithelial migration and gap closure during wound repair. What is new is the linkage of stress-induced death, debris clearance, and matrix or stromal guidance to repair, though none of these items test the interactions directly.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
